before:
Use JSON when storing a small record.
```javascript
const encoded = JSON.stringify({ color: "teal" });
console.log(encoded);
```
This produces text that can later be parsed.

This companion is for your own browser page, not the adapter tests.
```javascript
try {
  const storage = window.localStorage;
  storage.setItem("practice-color", JSON.stringify({ color: "teal" }));
  console.log(storage.getItem("practice-color"));
} catch (error) {
  console.log("Browser storage is unavailable");
}
```
Reload the same origin and read the same key to check persistence; remove the harmless practice key when finished.

after:
Use JSON to turn a small record into text before saving it.
```javascript
const encoded = JSON.stringify({ color: "teal" });
console.log(encoded);
```
Output:
```text
{"color":"teal"}
```
Later, `JSON.parse` can turn that text back into a value.

This separate example uses storage on a browser page you control.
```javascript
try {
  const storage = window.localStorage;
  storage.setItem("practice-color", JSON.stringify({ color: "teal" }));
  console.log(storage.getItem("practice-color"));
} catch (error) {
  console.log("Browser storage is unavailable");
}
```
It prints the saved JSON, or a message if storage is blocked. To check persistence, reload that same page and read the same key. Remove the practice key when finished.
